1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,820
Viral hepatitis is inflammation of the liver parenchyma.

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:04,880
It's usually due to hepatitis virus,

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:08,600
but it's important to note that other causes include EBV and CMV.

4
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:12,180
Recall from our discussion of infectious mononucleosis,

5
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,200
which was caused by EBV or CMV,

6
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:18,880
that you could also get a hepatitis, and I discussed that there.

7
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:21,900
Now our discussion here will focus on hepatitis virus,

8
00:00:22,280 --> 00:00:23,920
and this can cause an acute hepatitis,

9
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:26,140
which may progress to chronic hepatitis.

10
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:29,020
Patients with acute hepatitis present with jaundice,

11
00:00:29,020 --> 00:00:32,040
and this is due to a mixed conjugated bilirubinemia

12
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:34,660
and an unconjugated bilirubinemia.

13
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:39,340
Now the conjugated bilirubinemia is due to the fact that you destroy bile ductuals,

14
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:41,840
and so some of the conjugated bilirubin leaks out.

15
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:46,280
And the unconjugated bilirubinemia is due to the fact that you destroy hepatocytes,

16
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:49,140
and so you decrease the conjugating ability of the liver.

17
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:51,460
Now these patients will also have dark urine,

18
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:55,520
and the reason they have dark urine is because the conjugated bilirubin

19
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:59,480
is water-soluble and will be filtered by the kidney into the urine.

20
00:00:59,960 --> 00:01:02,860
The patients can also have fever, malaise, and nausea,

21
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:06,760
and these are some of the signs of the fact that they've got inflammation within the liver.

22
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:10,040
On laboratories, there will be elevated liver enzymes.

23
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:13,980
Classically, the ALT will be greater than the AST,

24
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:17,720
and the symptoms of acute hepatitis usually last less than six months.

25
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:20,380
And that's important because the presence of symptoms

26
00:01:20,380 --> 00:01:23,660
and appropriate serologic markers for greater than six months

27
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:26,600
would equal chronic hepatitis, as we'll discuss in a moment.

28
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:30,260
When you have acute hepatitis, you get inflammation within two areas.

29
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:32,600
There's inflammation within the portal tracts,

30
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:37,100
and also inflammation between the hepatocytes within the lobules of the liver.

31
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:40,760
This image here highlights inflammation between the hepatocytes,

32
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:43,000
and you can see all of these hepatocytes here,

33
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:47,220
and then you can clearly see the inflammatory infiltrate in between the hepatocytes.

34
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:50,200
Now, recall that if the hepatocytes are infected with virus,

35
00:01:50,540 --> 00:01:53,960
they're then going to present abnormal proteins on their MHC class 1.

36
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:57,760
That MHC class 1 will be recognized by CD8-positive T cells,

37
00:01:58,140 --> 00:02:00,620
and so the hepatocytes will undergo cytotoxic killing.

38
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:03,780
Of course, that would result in apoptosis of the hepatocytes,

39
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:08,240
and that's the mechanism of the destruction of the hepatocytes in acute hepatitis.

40
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:12,540
Chronic hepatitis is defined as the presence of symptoms in appropriate serology

41
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:14,860
that lasts for greater than six months.

42
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:18,240
Of course, chronic hepatitis has a risk of progression to cirrhosis,

43
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:20,560
and we'll hear more about that when we discuss cirrhosis.

44
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:23,120
Important to note that in chronic hepatitis,

45
00:02:23,220 --> 00:02:25,720
the inflammation predominantly involves the portal tracts,

46
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:28,660
and so here I'm outlining a portal tract,

47
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:32,200
and you can see that within the lobule of the liver here and here,

48
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:33,620
we don't really see much inflammation,

49
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:35,380
but clearly within the portal tract,

50
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:38,520
you can see all these small blue dots representing inflammatory cells.

51
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:42,000
There are multiple hepatitis viruses that can cause hepatitis.

52
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:45,460
The first two that we'll talk about are hepatitis A and hepatitis E.

53
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:47,940
These have a fecal-oral transmission.

54
00:02:48,460 --> 00:02:51,020
Hepatitis A is classically acquired by travelers,

55
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:56,580
and hepatitis E is classically acquired from contaminated water or undercooked seafood.

56
00:02:57,080 --> 00:02:59,020
Now, the reason why I bunch these two together

57
00:02:59,020 --> 00:03:02,840
is because they both produce acute hepatitis with no chronic state.

58
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:05,760
The presence of IgM marks active infection,

59
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:09,640
and the presence of IgG indicates that the patient has resolved the infection

60
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:11,260
or has been immunized.

61
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:15,440
Now, remember that immunization is only available for hepatitis A virus.

62
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:20,340
Very high yield to note that hepatitis E infection in a pregnant woman

63
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:22,720
is associated with fulminant hepatitis,

64
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:25,780
where the patients get liver failure with massive liver necrosis,

65
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:27,920
and this is particularly high yield to note.

66
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:31,180
Again, hepatitis E in a pregnant woman is highly worrisome.

67
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:34,880
Hepatitis B virus is transmitted parentally,

68
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:40,040
which basically means that it is transmitted via piercing of the skin or the mucous membranes.

69
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:43,880
So this can occur during childbirth, during unprotected intercourse,

70
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:45,520
or during IV drug abuse.

71
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:48,640
Hepatitis B classically results in acute hepatitis

72
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:51,160
and only rarely progresses to chronic disease.

73
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:54,960
Only 20% of cases will actually go on to result in chronic disease.

74
00:03:55,660 --> 00:03:59,260
When a patient has a hepatitis B infection, they can present in varying stages.

75
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:02,720
For example, the acute phase or the resolved phase, etc.

76
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:08,440
And serology is particularly useful for subclassifying these different stages.

77
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:11,100
And so we've got to spend a little bit of time on this chart,

78
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:12,600
and it's particularly high yield.

79
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:15,960
Now, the way by which I'm going to do this is to just introduce to you

80
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:17,400
some very simple principles,

81
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:19,780
and hopefully those principles will take you a long way.

82
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:23,520
The first principle is that hepatitis B's surface antigen

83
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:26,040
is the key marker of infection.

84
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:31,820
First of all, it is the first serologic marker to rise during the acute phase.

85
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:36,980
When the patient resolves the acute phase and resolves the infection, it disappears.

86
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:40,380
And if it is present for greater than six months,

87
00:04:40,680 --> 00:04:42,760
then that defines the chronic state.

88
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:44,740
Principle number two,

89
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:49,720
the key marker of acute battle is IgM against the core.

90
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:53,420
And the way I think about this is that the core is the core of the matter,

91
00:04:53,580 --> 00:04:54,480
at least serologically.

92
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:58,600
And so, here you have hepatitis B core antibody,

93
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:03,080
and you can see that during the acute battle for the first six months,

94
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:07,560
it is the major antibody that you use to try to defeat the virus.

95
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:10,640
Now, what happens during the acute phase is you produce the IgM,

96
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:13,540
and it hammers away, hammers away, hammers away at the virus,

97
00:05:13,860 --> 00:05:15,740
until it eventually knocks out the virus,

98
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:17,440
creating something called the window phase.

99
00:05:17,840 --> 00:05:20,920
The only thing that will be positive during the window phase will be IgM.

100
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:24,360
And then, towards the end of that battle, IgG will be produced.

101
00:05:24,580 --> 00:05:28,260
If you have permanently destroyed the surface antigen and other markers of the virus,

102
00:05:28,580 --> 00:05:30,180
then you'll call that a resolved infection.

103
00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:33,720
However, again, if surface antigen is present for greater than six months,

104
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:36,900
then that would indicate that the patient now has chronic disease.

105
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:42,900
Principle number three is that the sign of victory is the presence of IgG against the surface.

106
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:46,360
And so, here you have hepatitis B surface antibody.

107
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:49,760
You get IgG only if the infection resolves.

108
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:54,380
And if you go into the chronic state, you will not develop IgG against the surface.

109
00:05:55,040 --> 00:06:00,200
And so, again, the presence of IgG versus the surface indicates that you have won the battle.

110
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:03,500
Now, there is one other way to win the battle, and that would be to give immunization.

111
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:09,400
Remember that in immunization, we simply give the surface antigen to the patient with nothing else,

112
00:06:09,760 --> 00:06:12,760
and they'll produce an IgG against the surface antigen,

113
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:15,500
and then will be protected against the virus.

114
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:19,280
One other principle to note is that whenever you have IgG against the surface,

115
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:23,800
it also protects you, as is clearly illustrated by the notion of the immunization.

116
00:06:24,340 --> 00:06:26,660
Now, the final point, and the final high-yield point,

117
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:29,980
is that the envelope antigen indicates infectivity.

118
00:06:29,980 --> 00:06:33,640
So, here's the envelope antigen, and it's present during the acute phase.

119
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:35,500
It may be present during the chronic phase.

120
00:06:35,820 --> 00:06:39,820
But whenever it's present, it indicates that the virus can be transmitted to another person.

121
00:06:40,540 --> 00:06:44,840
Now, that's not hard to remember, because if you mail a letter to a friend, you need an envelope.

122
00:06:45,380 --> 00:06:49,680
And if the virus needs to go to another person, it's going to need the envelope antigen.

123
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:51,420
So, that's the way I remember that.

124
00:06:51,940 --> 00:06:55,680
Now, moving along to hepatitis C virus, this is also transmitted parentally,

125
00:06:55,680 --> 00:06:59,960
which basically means that it occurs through a nick in the skin or the mucous membranes.

126
00:07:00,300 --> 00:07:04,340
And, of course, that could occur with IV drug abuse, unprotected intercourse, or needle sticks.

127
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:08,720
The risk from transfusion of blood products is almost non-existent,

128
00:07:09,020 --> 00:07:10,920
and that's because we screen the blood supply.

129
00:07:11,460 --> 00:07:16,260
Hepatitis C will result in acute hepatitis, but it often progresses to chronic disease.

130
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:19,400
The key serology is hepatitis C virus RNA.

131
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:23,620
The presence of RNA confirms infection during the recovery.

132
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:28,960
The RNA levels will go down, and the persistence of RNA indicates chronic disease.

133
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:31,860
Now, the final discussion here is the hepatitis D virus.

134
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:35,540
The only thing you have to know here is that hepatitis D cannot infect by itself.

135
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:38,300
It requires hepatitis B for infection.

136
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:41,160
And there are two possible ways by which this can occur.

137
00:07:41,640 --> 00:07:46,640
The first is that a patient has a pre-existing hepatitis B virus infection,

138
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:50,920
and then they subsequently get hepatitis D on top of that prior B.

139
00:07:51,240 --> 00:07:52,860
That is called superinfection.

140
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:58,600
The second possibility is that the patient gets hepatitis B and hepatitis D together at the same time.

141
00:07:58,860 --> 00:08:00,080
That's called co-infection.

142
00:08:00,420 --> 00:08:02,600
What you have to know, especially for board examinations,

143
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:07,000
is that superinfection upon a pre-existing hepatitis B virus

144
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:11,300
is more severe than co-infection with B and D at the same time.

145
00:08:11,760 --> 00:08:13,860
And that's the last point from this particular table.

